window.INTRO={"voice": "af_heart", "total": 320.1, "scenes": [{"id": "welcome", "title": "Welcome", "text": "Hey hey! I'm Mrs. Francis, Trader Fox's partner, and I'm going to be your teacher. Trader Fox built this school for people who already know the game: risk, weight, timing, who to trust. I'm going to walk you through how all of that turns into trading. In the next few minutes you'll learn what trading actually is, how prices move, what a chart is telling you, why risk matters more than being right, and where to go next. No hype, no promises, and nobody running game on you. Let's go.", "audio": "video/intro-01.mp3", "dur": 25.83}, {"id": "what", "title": "What trading is", "text": "Let's start on the block. You pick up a unit for forty cents and move it for sixty. That's twenty cents of profit. Trading is the same idea, with a price that keeps changing. You buy something hoping to sell it for more. Or you sell first and buy it back cheaper, which is called going short. Either way, you only make money if the price moves your way.", "audio": "video/intro-02.mp3", "dur": 20.48}, {"id": "why", "title": "Why prices move", "text": "So why does price move? It's a tug of war on the block. When more people want to buy than sell, buyers have to pay a little more to get served, so price goes up. When more people want to sell, sellers have to take a little less, so price goes down. A chart is just the record of that tug of war, one moment at a time.", "audio": "video/intro-03.mp3", "dur": 18.98}, {"id": "nasdaq", "title": "The Nasdaq", "text": "So what are we trading? The Nasdaq is a stock exchange in New York, a marketplace where shares of companies are bought and sold. Think of it as one big side of town. The Nasdaq 100 is a list of one hundred of its biggest non-financial companies, names like Apple, Microsoft and Nvidia. When people say the Nasdaq is up today, they usually mean this index. Careful though: the exchange, the Composite, and the Nasdaq 100 are three different things.", "audio": "video/intro-04.mp3", "dur": 26.32}, {"id": "futures", "title": "Futures and the money math", "text": "You can't buy the index itself, but you can trade a futures contract that follows it, a deal locked in for later. This school uses the Micro E-mini Nasdaq 100, or M N Q. It's the small bag. Here's the key math. One point of movement is worth two dollars per contract, and the smallest step, a quarter point, is fifty cents. If price moves ten points your way on one contract, that's twenty dollars. Ten points against you is minus twenty. Small numbers, which is why many people start there. Count the money before the move, not after.", "audio": "video/intro-05.mp3", "dur": 31.7}, {"id": "candle", "title": "Reading a candle", "text": "Charts are made of candles. Each candle is one round of the block, written down. It shows four prices for a slice of time: where it opened, the highest point, the lowest point, and where it closed. If it closed higher than it opened, it's green. If it closed lower, it's red. The thin lines are wicks, and they show how far price went before somebody got turned away. One candle tells you very little. Many candles in a row tell a story.", "audio": "video/intro-06.mp3", "dur": 25.18}, {"id": "frames", "title": "Timeframes", "text": "A candle can cover one minute, five minutes, an hour, or a whole day. That's the timeframe, how close you're standing. Zoom out to a bigger timeframe to see the trend and the big levels, like looking at the whole neighborhood. Zoom in to a smaller one to time your entry. Real traders flip between them all the time, and the practice game lets you do exactly that.", "audio": "video/intro-07.mp3", "dur": 21.15}, {"id": "structure", "title": "Trends and levels", "text": "Markets move in waves. An uptrend is higher highs and higher lows, like climbing stairs, a block getting busier. A downtrend is the opposite. Traders also mark levels where price turned before: a floor called support, and a ceiling called resistance. Think of them as corners with a history, because lots of people are watching the same spots. Mark them before the session starts, not after the move.", "audio": "video/intro-08.mp3", "dur": 24.18}, {"id": "patterns", "title": "Patterns, honestly", "text": "You'll hear names like fair value gap, liquidity sweep, and breakout. They're real vocabulary, and the guides in this school show each one move by move. A sweep is basically getting set up and robbed on your stop. But here's the honest part: for many of these ideas, independent evidence is thin. So learn to spot them, then test them yourself, costs included. A pattern is a question to ask, not a promise.", "audio": "video/intro-09.mp3", "dur": 23.93}, {"id": "risk", "title": "Risk: the real game", "text": "Here's the most important idea in this video. Before you enter any trade, decide where you're wrong. That's your stop, the line where you walk. And decide where you'll take profit. That's your target. If you risk one dollar to make two, you only need to win about one trade in three to break even, before costs. Then size the trade so a loss is small enough that you can take it again and again. You already know that carrying too much weight on a bad day ends you.", "audio": "video/intro-10.mp3", "dur": 25.45}, {"id": "truth", "title": "The honest truth", "text": "Now the real talk. In one big study of Brazilian futures day traders, ninety seven percent of those who kept going for more than three hundred days lost money. In a Taiwan study, fewer than one percent were predictably profitable after costs. That's not here to scare you. It's here so you take risk, practice, and patience seriously, and never trade money you can't afford to lose. Anybody who promises you guaranteed profits is running game on you.", "audio": "video/intro-11.mp3", "dur": 26.8}, {"id": "prop", "title": "Prop firms", "text": "Some people trade through a prop firm. Think of it as getting fronted. You pay a fee, pass an evaluation by hitting a profit target without breaking a loss limit, and then trade the firm's money. The rules are strict, and they differ from firm to firm and change over time, so read them line by line. Cross the line they draw and you're cut off. The practice game here copies one popular set of evaluation rules, so you can feel that pressure for free.", "audio": "video/intro-12.mp3", "dur": 25.95}, {"id": "next", "title": "What to do next", "text": "So what now? Take the lessons in order, they build on each other. Play the practice game on real market history, the dry run, and use Spot it to train your eyes. Check My habits to see your own mistakes, keep your book, and use Street talk any time a word trips you up. Remember, this is education only, not financial advice. Welcome to Trader Fox's Trading School, Street Edition. I'm Mrs. Francis, and I'll see you inside.", "audio": "video/intro-13.mp3", "dur": 24.18}]};
